00:35:55.080 --> 00:35:59.400
is they make you, the player, fear
death in a medium where death is

696
00:35:59.400 --> 00:36:00.560
by definition, impermanent.

697
00:36:00.560 --> 00:36:02.800
You're always going to respawn,
you're always going to come back.

698
00:36:02.800 --> 00:36:05.320
So fundamentally
the player knows they will not die.

699
00:36:05.320 --> 00:36:07.280
That is absolutely not true of the NPCs.

700
00:36:07.280 --> 00:36:09.440
This was the least spoiler image
I chose to find

701
00:36:09.440 --> 00:36:12.280
because we've been mad about death
for 30 years.

702
00:36:12.280 --> 00:36:15.320
You can't kill the player,
but you can kill the NPCs,

703
00:36:15.320 --> 00:36:17.000
and if your player cares about them,
you can't.

704
00:36:17.000 --> 00:36:18.680
A lot of emotional half out of that.

705
00:36:18.680 --> 00:36:22.200
So short developers can use non-player
characters, and particularly groups

706
00:36:22.200 --> 00:36:25.880
or communities of non-player characters,
as a way to make the world feel real

707
00:36:25.880 --> 00:36:27.000
and a world that you care about.

708
00:36:27.000 --> 00:36:30.000
And from there, we can make that world
also very, very scary.

709
00:36:30.080 --> 00:36:34.720
So very briefly, I'm going to talk about

710
00:36:34.800 --> 00:36:36.680
one of the three ways
in which we can do this,

711
00:36:36.680 --> 00:36:39.440
which is to emphasize the hostility
and danger of the game space.

712
00:36:39.440 --> 00:36:42.480
So broadly, scary games
take place in scary environments.

713
00:36:42.560 --> 00:36:44.240
There are lots of different kinds
of environments

714
00:36:44.240 --> 00:36:45.920
that we can use to
heighten that experience.

715
00:36:45.920 --> 00:36:47.920
I'm going to talk primarily about two.

716
00:36:47.920 --> 00:36:51.880
The first is environments, 
and we can see this the history of horror

717
00:36:51.880 --> 00:36:54.000
and the history of Gothic environments
that are scary

718
00:36:54.000 --> 00:36:55.880
because they should be populated
but aren't.

719
00:36:55.880 --> 00:36:59.640
So the abandoned hospital, the abandoned
shopping mall, the abandoned school.

720
00:36:59.640 --> 00:37:02.840
And these environments are uncanny
because of their abandonment.

721
00:37:02.840 --> 00:37:06.440
They're supposed to be full of a thriving,
happy, positive environment.

722
00:37:06.440 --> 00:37:08.240
And they're absolutely not.

723
00:37:08.240 --> 00:37:10.960
The abandoned
hospital has been overused at this point.

724
00:37:10.960 --> 00:37:14.040
I've been in so many derelict hospitals,
so hang on to this for a minute.

725
00:37:14.040 --> 00:37:15.320
We'll come back to it.

726
00:37:15.320 --> 00:37:17.800
The second one,
as a counterpoint, is the environment.

727
00:37:17.800 --> 00:37:18.720
That is scary.

728
00:37:18.720 --> 00:37:21.720
Not because it's been abandoned by humans,
but because it should not have humans

729
00:37:21.720 --> 00:37:22.560
in it at all.

730
00:37:22.560 --> 00:37:26.600
So a great example of
this is the alien ocean in Subnautica.

731
00:37:26.600 --> 00:37:29.760
So very, very briefly, 
the premise of Subnautica

732
00:37:29.760 --> 00:37:32.960
is that you are Robinson Crusoe
ING your way through an alien ocean.

733
00:37:32.960 --> 00:37:34.720
The game starts
with the destruction of the ship,

734
00:37:34.720 --> 00:37:37.200
the Aurora,
and you land on this ocean by yourself.

735
00:37:37.200 --> 00:37:38.400
The first 30 minutes of the game,

736
00:37:38.400 --> 00:37:40.960
you have to deal with an environment
that will kill you, the player.

737
00:37:40.960 --> 00:37:46.040
By default it is 95% underwater
and drowning is a major gaming panic.

738
00:37:46.040 --> 00:37:49.920
The environment is in 360 degrees,
so you have to be aware of danger

739
00:37:49.920 --> 00:37:52.920
that can come at you from literally
any angle which humans are not great at.

740
00:37:52.920 --> 00:37:55.000
And you are.

741
00:37:55.000 --> 00:37:57.520
I really wish I hadn't put this image up
because I still have nightmares

742
00:37:57.520 --> 00:37:58.160
about that creature.

743
00:37:58.160 --> 00:38:00.320
This is absolutely the scariest game
I've ever played.

744
00:38:00.320 --> 00:38:04.080
So Nautica is full of an entire ecology
of creatures that consider you nowhere

745
00:38:04.080 --> 00:38:06.480
near the top of the food chain,
and there is no combat system,

746
00:38:06.480 --> 00:38:09.200
so if you see one,
you're pretty much dead.

747
00:38:09.200 --> 00:38:13.560
So the structure of the environment
in Subnautica by itself does a

748
00:38:13.560 --> 00:38:17.200
really good job of emphasizing the horror
and the difficulty of this space.

749
00:38:17.480 --> 00:38:19.280
And this is also, again, that has

750
00:38:19.280 --> 00:38:23.200
no other characters that you ever see,
but you do hear them quite a bit.

751
00:38:23.240 --> 00:38:26.080
So one of the ways that Sonata uses

752
00:38:26.080 --> 00:38:30.160
the community of NPCs to heighten
that horror is with the radio.

753
00:38:30.160 --> 00:38:33.240
So once you've crashed very quickly,
you get access to radio

754
00:38:33.240 --> 00:38:34.600
and you start hearing distress signals

755
00:38:34.600 --> 00:38:37.040
from all of the other survivors
that have made it.

756
00:38:37.040 --> 00:38:38.520
So you're getting essentially voice lines.

757
00:38:38.520 --> 00:38:39.920
You're getting data about where they are,

758
00:38:39.920 --> 00:38:44.000
and the narrative of the game forces
you to go in those directions.

759
00:38:44.000 --> 00:38:46.200
You can't stop and ignore it.

760
00:38:46.200 --> 00:38:49.560
You have to to progress, to go to these
places that the survivors have come to.

761
00:38:50.560 --> 00:38:53.400
So each time you find a life pod,
essentially

762
00:38:53.400 --> 00:38:56.280
you're finding the after effects
of a horror movie that's already happened.

763
00:38:56.280 --> 00:38:57.760
You never see another character

764
00:38:57.760 --> 00:39:00.760
that you hear them trying to survive,
and then you find them after they happen.

765
00:39:00.760 --> 00:39:04.480
So what this does is it emphasizes
both immediately and over the long

766
00:39:04.520 --> 00:39:06.560
term, the horror of the space immediately.

767
00:39:06.560 --> 00:39:07.920
It's this could have been made.

768
00:39:07.920 --> 00:39:10.120
This could have happened to me long term.

769
00:39:10.120 --> 00:39:12.960
It emphasizes the horror,
because those signals will die out,

770
00:39:12.960 --> 00:39:17.160
that as you continually fail to rescue
people, you gradually get to the point

771
00:39:17.160 --> 00:39:19.720
where you're not getting any more signals
in your left all alone.

772
00:39:19.720 --> 00:39:23.400
And I will say, from a technical design
standpoint, I also love this as a

773
00:39:23.640 --> 00:39:26.040
as a choice by the developers,
because part of the reason

774
00:39:26.040 --> 00:39:26.920
to make this choice

775
00:39:26.920 --> 00:39:29.320
that you hear the survivors
that you never managed to see them,

776
00:39:29.320 --> 00:39:32.080
is because they didn't have a character
animator on staff and knew they couldn't

777
00:39:32.080 --> 00:39:33.840
actually create human beings.

778
00:39:33.840 --> 00:39:38.200
But back to the idea of the hostile game
space that should have a lot of people.

779
00:39:38.200 --> 00:39:41.600
So Crow country itself,
it takes place in an abandoned

780
00:39:41.600 --> 00:39:44.600
amusement park, a space that should have
a lot of happy living people in it.

781
00:39:44.600 --> 00:39:48.160
And very quickly, the first thing you run
into is a wounded teenager.

782
00:39:48.160 --> 00:39:49.160
At that.

783
00:39:49.160 --> 00:39:51.560
I will say the game forces you to save.

784
00:39:51.560 --> 00:39:54.920
You cannot but save Arthur mode,
but the way that it presents it

785
00:39:54.920 --> 00:39:57.000
very early, when you don't know
the literacy of the space,

786
00:39:57.000 --> 00:39:58.960
when you're not quite sure
what the game will

787
00:39:58.960 --> 00:40:01.880
and will not allow you to do,
it feels like a very real choice.

788
00:40:01.880 --> 00:40:03.920
You have so right up front,
the game is reminding you

789
00:40:03.920 --> 00:40:06.040
people can die in this space.
It's not necessarily you,

790
00:40:06.040 --> 00:40:08.160
but there are people
you're going to learn to care about.

791
00:40:08.160 --> 00:40:11.200
I realize I see this as the problem
with putting Subnautica on a talk.

792
00:40:11.200 --> 00:40:12.520
I'll talk about it forever.

793
00:40:12.520 --> 00:40:16.240
To skip through this,
let's just say that there are 5 to 8

794
00:40:16.280 --> 00:40:18.120
non-player characters that you see

795
00:40:18.120 --> 00:40:20.120
within Crow country,
and the way that the written you do

796
00:40:20.120 --> 00:40:21.320
get to care about all of them.

797
00:40:21.320 --> 00:40:23.160
They do have their own community
that they form.

798
00:40:23.160 --> 00:40:26.280
They have a lot of thoughts
about each other, about how they act.

799
00:40:26.320 --> 00:40:29.760
You can't save them all, but there are
five that you can get at the end.

800
00:40:29.840 --> 00:40:33.280
But the real community
that you're dealing with is the guests.

801
00:40:33.280 --> 00:40:35.920
And here's where I'm just going to say
very briefly that if you've not played

802
00:40:35.920 --> 00:40:39.960
Crow Country Mute for about five minutes,
my apologies for this,

803
00:40:39.960 --> 00:40:41.960
because the most interesting thing
happens at the end,

804
00:40:41.960 --> 00:40:44.400
which is a pair of subsequent reveals.

805
00:40:44.400 --> 00:40:46.760
And this is where player
agency is very much subverted.

806
00:40:46.760 --> 00:40:50.440
So the first reveal is not unusual
for a horror game.

807
00:40:50.480 --> 00:40:53.840
Of course, the mutated, vaguely
humanoid creatures that you're fighting,

808
00:40:53.880 --> 00:40:55.440
of course, they're actually humans.

809
00:40:55.440 --> 00:40:57.680
Of course
that's that's where we're going with this.

810
00:40:57.680 --> 00:41:00.320
So that's not going to hit
hard for a horror game player.

811
00:41:00.320 --> 00:41:03.760
The second reveal after
that is where those humans come from.

812
00:41:03.760 --> 00:41:05.600
And this is where the game
is subverting agency,

813
00:41:05.600 --> 00:41:09.520
because you have the first reveal
of everything that you fought and killed

814
00:41:09.520 --> 00:41:10.880
and tried very hard not to die to.

815
00:41:10.880 --> 00:41:12.880
Everything you've decimated over
the course of the game

816
00:41:12.880 --> 00:41:16.600
was actually a human being
that maybe potentially could be saved.

817
00:41:16.640 --> 00:41:21.040
The second reveal is that those humans
are actually coming from the future.

818
00:41:21.040 --> 00:41:22.600
The game takes place in 1990.

819
00:41:22.600 --> 00:41:27.560
They are coming from the year 2106,
and they are essentially coming back

820
00:41:27.600 --> 00:41:28.760
to the year 1990

821
00:41:28.760 --> 00:41:31.480
to try to prevent a major catastrophe
that's happened in the future.

822
00:41:31.480 --> 00:41:34.800
This game is not subtle
about climate change, and it's not subtle

823
00:41:34.800 --> 00:41:37.000
about the fact that the reason
those humans are coming through

824
00:41:37.000 --> 00:41:39.920
mutated is because of things
that are happening in 1990.

825
00:41:39.920 --> 00:41:43.960
But the point that matters here
is that you, the player, you can't fix it,

826
00:41:44.000 --> 00:41:46.360
which is a huge subversion of agency
and games.

827
00:41:46.360 --> 00:41:50.560
We are trained that all problems
are solvable and all problems are ours

828
00:41:50.560 --> 00:41:51.320
to solve.

829
00:41:51.320 --> 00:41:54.320
And the whole idea that there's
this massive problem in the future,

830
00:41:54.360 --> 00:41:57.200
like normally
we would be the ones to deal with that.

831
00:41:57.200 --> 00:42:00.600
The developers have said about
Crow Country that it is in some ways

832
00:42:00.640 --> 00:42:03.760
a game about being born
just too late to save the world.

833
00:42:03.760 --> 00:42:05.400
And again,
when we get to the very end of it,

834
00:42:05.400 --> 00:42:09.280
which I'll just skip to with this slide,
what you see is the five people

835
00:42:09.280 --> 00:42:10.360
that have managed to come out,

836
00:42:10.360 --> 00:42:12.920
including the player,
and none of them are happy about it

837
00:42:12.920 --> 00:42:14.960
because we've survived
this one moment of horror.

838
00:42:14.960 --> 00:42:16.920
But we know what's coming,
and we know we are

839
00:42:16.920 --> 00:42:20.080
not only unable to stop it,
but have been unable to stop it.

840
00:42:20.080 --> 00:42:23.240
And again, it's centered
not just to round those five characters

841
00:42:23.240 --> 00:42:26.640
and not even about the larger
group of characters in the amusement park,

842
00:42:26.680 --> 00:42:29.120
but about the community of humanity
as a whole.

843
00:42:29.120 --> 00:42:30.640
So, briefly,

844
00:42:30.640 --> 00:42:33.840
the reason that I care about this
is that I am consistently interested

845
00:42:33.840 --> 00:42:36.880
in the kinds of stories that are best
or only told through interactive media

846
00:42:36.880 --> 00:42:38.040
and through the medium of games.

847
00:42:38.040 --> 00:42:41.120
And one of the things
that's interesting about horror games

848
00:42:41.120 --> 00:42:43.560
specifically, and indie horror
in this particular case,

849
00:42:43.560 --> 00:42:47.440
is how we're not now pulling from film
or from cinema from other places,

850
00:42:47.440 --> 00:42:50.840
but looking at how we can actually scare
the player effectively through mechanics

851
00:42:50.840 --> 00:42:51.560
and through agency.

852
00:42:51.560 --> 00:42:54.320
And I'm really interested to see how
these indie horror games are doing that.

853
00:42:54.320 --> 00:42:56.640
And that is now
at the end of this part of this talk.

854
00:42:56.640 --> 00:42:57.800
Thank you very, very much.
